<section xml:id="sec-mod9-worked-examples">
  <title>Worked Examples</title>
  <p>Estimated time: 45 minutes.</p>

    <p>
      Read each example slowly. <xref ref="ex-mod9-we-1"/> dissects the canonical non-uniform
      example <m>f_n(x) = x^n</m>. <xref ref="ex-mod9-we-2"/> proves the uniform-limit
      continuity theorem using the <m>3\varepsilon</m> trick. <xref ref="ex-mod9-we-3"/>
      applies the Weierstrass <m>M</m>-Test. <xref ref="ex-mod9-we-4"/> uses term-by-term
      integration to derive a classical series identity.
    </p>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 1
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod9-we-1">
    <title>Pointwise but Not Uniform Convergence</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f_n(x) = x^n</m> for <m>x \in [0,1]</m>. Show that
        <m>(f_n)</m> converges pointwise but <em>not</em> uniformly on
        <m>[0,1]</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
        For pointwise convergence, fix <m>x</m> and compute
        <m>\lim_{n \to \infty} x^n</m>. For non-uniform convergence, compute
        the sup-norm <m>\sup_{x \in [0,1]} |f_n(x) - f(x)|</m> and show it
        does not go to <m>0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Pointwise limit.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>x \in [0,1)</m>: <m>|x| \lt 1</m>, so
        <m>x^n \to 0</m> as <m>n \to \infty</m>.
        For <m>x = 1</m>: <m>1^n = 1</m> for all <m>n</m>, so
        <m>x^n \to 1</m>.
        Define <m>f(x) = 0</m> for <m>x \in [0,1)</m> and <m>f(1) = 1</m>.
        Then <m>f_n \to f</m> pointwise on <m>[0,1]</m>.
        Note that <m>f</m> is <em>discontinuous</em> at <m>x = 1</m>,
        even though each <m>f_n</m> is continuous on <m>[0,1]</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Non-uniform convergence (sup-norm argument).</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        For any <m>n \in \N</m> and any <m>x \in [0,1)</m>,
        <m>|f_n(x) - f(x)| = x^n</m>. As <m>x \to 1^-</m>,
        <m>x^n \to 1</m>. Therefore
        <md>
          \sup_{x \in [0,1]} |f_n(x) - f(x)| = 1
        </md>
        for every <m>n</m>. (Every value <m>|f_n(x) - f(x)|</m> is at most
        <m>1</m>: it equals <m>x^n \lt 1</m> for <m>x \in [0,1)</m> and
        <m>0</m> at <m>x = 1</m>. So the sup is at most <m>1</m>. It is not
        less than <m>1</m>, because <m>x^n \to 1</m> as <m>x \to 1^-</m>, so
        the values <m>x^n</m> with <m>x \in [0,1)</m> come arbitrarily close
        to <m>1</m>. The sup is not attained.) Since the sup is <m>1</m> for every
        <m>n</m>, it does not converge to <m>0</m>, and the convergence is
        not uniform. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        The failure of uniform convergence here has a visible consequence:
        the pointwise limit is discontinuous. The theorem in <xref ref="ex-mod9-we-2"/> says
        this cannot happen under uniform convergence of continuous functions.
        The non-uniform behavior of <m>x^n</m> on <m>[0,1]</m> is the
        prototypical counterexample for this entire module.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 2
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod9-we-2">
    <title>Uniform Convergence Preserves Continuity</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove: if <m>f_n \to f</m> uniformly on <m>[a,b]</m> and each
        <m>f_n</m> is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m>, then <m>f</m> is
        continuous on <m>[a,b]</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
        Fix <m>x_0 \in [a,b]</m> and <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>. Split
        <m>|f(x) - f(x_0)|</m> into three pieces using the triangle
        inequality, and control each piece separately.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Since <m>f_n \to f</m> uniformly, there exists <m>N \in \N</m>
        such that for all <m>n \geq N</m> and all <m>x \in [a,b]</m>,
        <m>|f_n(x) - f(x)| \lt \varepsilon/3</m>.
        Fix such an <m>N</m>; the function <m>f_N</m> is continuous at
        <m>x_0</m>, so there exists <m>\delta \gt 0</m> such that
        <m>|x - x_0| \lt \delta \Rightarrow |f_N(x) - f_N(x_0)| \lt
        \varepsilon/3</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now suppose <m>|x - x_0| \lt \delta</m>. By the triangle inequality,
        <md>
          <mrow>|f(x) - f(x_0)| \amp \leq |f(x) - f_N(x)| + |f_N(x) -
          f_N(x_0)| + |f_N(x_0) - f(x_0)|</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp \lt \frac{\varepsilon}{3} + \frac{\varepsilon}{3} +
          \frac{\varepsilon}{3} = \varepsilon.</mrow>
        </md>
        Since <m>x_0</m> was arbitrary, <m>f</m> is continuous on
        <m>[a,b]</m>. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        This is the <m>3\varepsilon</m> trick. The three terms are controlled
        by: (1) uniform convergence applied at <m>x</m>; (2) continuity of
        <m>f_N</m>; (3) uniform convergence applied at <m>x_0</m>. The key
        is that <m>N</m> is chosen <em>before</em> <m>x</m>&#x2014;this is
        exactly what uniform convergence buys. With only pointwise convergence,
        the <m>N</m> from (1) would depend on <m>x</m>, and no single
        <m>f_N</m> (and so no single <m>\delta</m>) would work for every
        <m>x</m> near <m>x_0</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 3
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod9-we-3">
    <title>The Weierstrass <m>M</m>-Test</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{\sin(nx)}{n^2}</m>
        converges absolutely and uniformly on <m>\R</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
        Find a bound <m>M_n</m> independent of <m>x</m> with
        <m>|f_n(x)| \leq M_n</m> and <m>\sum M_n \lt \infty</m>.
        Then invoke the Weierstrass <m>M</m>-Test.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Set <m>f_n(x) = \sin(nx)/n^2</m>. For all <m>x \in \R</m> and all
        <m>n \in \N</m>,
        <md>
          |f_n(x)| = \frac{|\sin(nx)|}{n^2} \leq \frac{1}{n^2} =: M_n.
        </md>
        The series <m>\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} M_n = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}
        \frac{1}{n^2}</m> is a convergent <m>p</m>-series (<m>p = 2 \gt 1</m>).
        By the Weierstrass <m>M</m>-Test,
        <m>\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \sin(nx)/n^2</m> converges absolutely and
        uniformly on <m>\R</m>. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        The <m>M</m>-Test is essentially a comparison test for series of
        functions, with the dominating series <m>\sum M_n</m> playing the
        role of the known convergent series. The uniformity of the bound
        <m>|f_n(x)| \leq M_n</m> (no <m>x</m> in <m>M_n</m>) is what
        delivers uniform convergence. Notice also that we used
        <m>|\sin(nx)| \leq 1</m>, a bound that holds for every real
        <m>x</m>; that is why the same <m>M_n</m> works on the whole domain
        <m>\R</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 4
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod9-we-4">
    <title>Term-by-Term Integration</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the geometric series, the term-by-term integration theorem,
        and an estimate of the remainder to show that
        <md>
          \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^n}{2n+1} = \frac{\pi}{4}.
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
        Start from the geometric series for <m>1/(1+t^2)</m>, integrate
        term by term (justified by uniform convergence on a compact interval)
        to get the series for <m>\arctan(x)</m> when <m>0 \leq x \lt 1</m>,
        and then reach <m>x = 1</m> with a direct estimate of the remainder.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>|t| \lt 1</m>, the geometric series gives
        <md>
          \frac{1}{1 + t^2} = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} (-1)^n t^{2n}.
        </md>
        For any <m>0 \lt r \lt 1</m>, the series converges uniformly on
        <m>[-r, r]</m> by the Weierstrass <m>M</m>-Test (with
        <m>M_n = r^{2n}</m> and <m>\sum r^{2n} = 1/(1-r^2) \lt \infty</m>).
        Each partial-sum function is continuous, and the uniform limit of
        continuous functions is continuous (<xref ref="ex-mod9-we-2"/>). By the term-by-term
        integration theorem, for <m>x \in [0,r]</m>,
        <md>
          \int_0^x \frac{dt}{1+t^2} = \int_0^x
          \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} (-1)^n t^{2n}\,dt =
          \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} (-1)^n \frac{x^{2n+1}}{2n+1}.
        </md>
        The left side equals <m>\arctan(x)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Setting <m>x = 1</m> is not covered by this argument: the geometric
        series diverges at <m>t = 1</m>, so it does not converge uniformly
        on <m>[0,1]</m>. The series
        <m>\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^n}{2n+1}</m> does converge, by
        the Alternating Series Test (terms positive, decreasing to
        <m>0</m>), but that alone does not tell us its sum. We estimate the
        partial sums directly. For every <m>t</m> and every <m>n \geq 0</m>,
        the finite geometric sum
        <m>\sum_{k=0}^{n} (-t^2)^k = \bigl(1 - (-t^2)^{n+1}\bigr)/(1+t^2)</m>
        gives
        <md>
          \frac{1}{1+t^2} = \sum_{k=0}^{n} (-1)^k t^{2k} + (-1)^{n+1}\frac{t^{2n+2}}{1+t^2}.
        </md>
        Every function here is continuous on <m>[0,1]</m>, so we may
        integrate from <m>0</m> to <m>1</m> and use linearity and FTC Part 2
        (<xref ref="ch-mod8" text="custom">Module 8</xref>), with
        <m>\arctan(1) = \pi/4</m>:
        <md>
          \frac{\pi}{4} - \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{(-1)^k}{2k+1} = (-1)^{n+1}\int_0^1 \frac{t^{2n+2}}{1+t^2}\,dt.
        </md>
        On <m>[0,1]</m>, <m>0 \leq t^{2n+2}/(1+t^2) \leq t^{2n+2}</m>, so by
        monotonicity of the integral
        <md>
          \left|\frac{\pi}{4} - \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{(-1)^k}{2k+1}\right| \leq \int_0^1 t^{2n+2}\,dt = \frac{1}{2n+3}.
        </md>
        The right side tends to <m>0</m>, so the partial sums converge to
        <m>\pi/4</m>:
        <md>
          \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^n}{2n+1}
          = 1 - \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{5} - \cdots = \frac{\pi}{4}.
        </md>
        <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        The term-by-term integration theorem gives the series for
        <m>\arctan(x)</m> on any <m>[0,r]</m> with <m>r \lt 1</m>, but not at
        the endpoint <m>x = 1</m>, where the geometric series fails to
        converge. The value <m>\pi/4</m> at <m>x = 1</m> comes from the
        remainder estimate, not from the term-by-term integration theorem,
        which covers only intervals <m>[0,r]</m> with <m>r \lt 1</m>.
        A general result, Abel's theorem, says that whenever a
        power series converges at an endpoint of its interval of
        convergence, its value there is the limit of its values from inside
        (see Rudin, <pubtitle>Principles of Mathematical Analysis</pubtitle>,
        3rd edition, Theorem 8.2). Here a direct estimate suffices, and it
        even gives a rate: the <m>n</m>th partial sum is within
        <m>1/(2n+3)</m> of <m>\pi/4</m>. The same estimate, with
        <m>\int_0^x</m> in place of <m>\int_0^1</m>, proves the series for
        <m>\arctan(x)</m> for every <m>x \in [0,1]</m>. The punchline&#x2014;<m>\pi/4</m> as an alternating series
        of unit fractions&#x2014;is one of the most beautiful results in
        elementary analysis.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
